+
+## SQLite Storage
+
+The replacement database contains one registry-record table keyed by normalized serial number and indexed for exact lookup. Each row contains the original serial number, equipment description, calibration date, due date, and snapshot date. A database-level uniqueness constraint on normalized serial number defends the importer against duplicate records in addition to validation.
+
+The active database is opened read-only for lookups. Import preparation uses a separate staging database, so lookup requests continue to read the prior active database until activation. The importer closes all staging handles before atomic replacement. If the kiosk restarts before activation, the staged database is discarded and the last active database remains the registry.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|-------------------------|-----------|
+| Registry lookup backend | Embedded SQLite database on each kiosk | On-site HTTPS directory; managed search service | SQLite keeps lookup availability independent of the lab network and avoids service operation, credentials, and recurring external costs. Each kiosk accepts the bounded work of rebuilding and validating its own index during snapshot import. |
